{"version":"6.9","hitCount":1,"request":{"queryString":"TITLE:\"Using Big Data to Emulate a Target Trial\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":6,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"26994063","source":"MED","pmid":"26994063","pmcid":"PMC4832051","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC4832051"]},"doi":"10.1093/aje/kwv254","title":"Using Big Data to Emulate a Target Trial When a Randomized Trial Is Not Available.","authorString":"Hernán MA, Robins JM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hernán MA","lastName":"Hernán","firstName":"Miguel A","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1619-8456"}},{"fullName":"Robins JM","lastName":"Robins","firstName":"James M","initials":"JM"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1619-8456"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"8","volume":"183","journalIssueId":2409701,"dateOfPublication":"2016 Apr","monthOfPublication":4,"yearOfPublication":2016,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Am J Epidemiol","title":"American journal of epidemiology","essn":"1476-6256","issn":"0002-9262","isoabbreviation":"Am J Epidemiol","nlmid":"7910653"},"printPublicationDate":"2016-04-01"},"pubYear":"2016","pageInfo":"758-764","abstractText":"Ideally, questions about comparative effectiveness or safety would be answered using an appropriately designed and conducted randomized experiment. When we cannot conduct a randomized experiment, we analyze observational data. Causal inference from large observational databases (big data) can be viewed as an attempt to emulate a randomized experiment-the target experiment or target trial-that would answer the question of interest. When the goal is to guide decisions among several strategies, causal analyses of observational data need to be evaluated with respect to how well they emulate a particular target trial. We outline a framework for comparative effectiveness research using big data that makes the target trial explicit. This framework channels counterfactual theory for comparing the effects of sustained treatment strategies, organizes analytic approaches, provides a structured process for the criticism of observational studies, and helps avoid common methodologic pitfalls.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"P01 CA134294","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"R35 CA197449","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NCI NIH HHS","acronym":"CA","grantId":"P01 CA134294","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"R01 AI102634","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"R01 AI102634","orderIn":0},{"agency":"NIAID NIH HHS","acronym":"AI","grantId":"R37 AI032475","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Breast Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ET","qualifierName":"etiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"EP","qualifierName":"epidemiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Progestins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"TU","qualifierName":"therapeutic use"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Therapy, Combination"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Estrogen Replacement Therapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AE","qualifierName":"adverse effects"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"SN","qualifierName":"statistics & numerical data"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Causality"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Postmenopause"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Databases, Factual","meshQualifierList